WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.735
Borrowing lets code access a value without
becoming responsible for owning and

2
00:00:03.815 --> 00:00:04.443
cleaning it up.

3
00:00:04.523 --> 00:00:06.890
This is what makes Rust practical for
normal programming.

4
00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:09.239
Not every function should take ownership
of its inputs.

5
00:00:09.319 --> 00:00:12.458
Many functions only need to inspect data,
format it, validate it, compare it,

6
00:00:12.538 --> 00:00:15.387
or temporarily use it while the original
owner keeps responsibility.

7
00:00:15.467 --> 00:00:17.930
A shared reference gives read-only access
to borrowed data.

8
00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:21.342
A function that receives a shared
reference is communicating that it does not need

9
00:00:21.422 --> 00:00:23.450
ownership and does not intend to modify
the value.

10
00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:26.911
That makes the function easier to call and
easier to review because the caller knows

11
00:00:26.991 --> 00:00:29.309
the value remains available after the
function returns.

12
00:00:29.389 --> 00:00:31.562
A mutable reference gives controlled
modification.

13
00:00:31.642 --> 00:00:34.974
A function that receives a mutable
reference is saying it needs to change the value

14
00:00:35.054 --> 00:00:37.661
in place, but it still does not need to
own the value permanently.

15
00:00:37.741 --> 00:00:41.074
This is useful for updating buffers,
appending to collections, adjusting state,

16
00:00:41.154 --> 00:00:44.534
or performing transformations that should
remain attached to the original owner.

17
00:00:44.614 --> 00:00:46.352
Borrowing is therefore part of API design.

18
00:00:46.432 --> 00:00:48.509
A formatting function may take a shared
reference.

19
00:00:48.589 --> 00:00:51.052
A validation function may inspect data
without changing it.

20
00:00:51.132 --> 00:00:53.450
A parser may return owned data when it
creates a new value.

21
00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:56.669
A transformation function may take a
mutable reference when it should modify

22
00:00:56.749 --> 00:00:57.425
existing state.

23
00:00:57.505 --> 00:00:59.533
The signature communicates the
responsibility.

24
00:00:59.613 --> 00:01:02.559
Good Rust APIs usually take the least
powerful access that fits the task.

25
00:01:02.639 --> 00:01:05.150
If a function only needs to read, it
should not take ownership.

26
00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:06.727
If it only needs temporary mutation,

27
00:01:06.807 --> 00:01:08.931
it should not require the caller to give
up the value.

28
00:01:09.011 --> 00:01:11.716
These choices reduce unnecessary copies,
make data flow clearer,

29
00:01:11.796 --> 00:01:14.500
and help reviewers understand what each
function is allowed to do.
